      {
        title: "Economic Systems",
        content: "An ECONOMIC SYSTEM is the way a society organises the production, distribution, and consumption of goods and services to answer the three basic economic questions.\nA FREE MARKET ECONOMY (capitalism) relies on the price mechanism to allocate resources. Private individuals own the factors of production, and supply and demand determine prices, output, and distribution.\nAdvantages of a market economy: consumer sovereignty (consumers drive production through their spending choices), efficiency through competition, innovation incentive through profit motive, freedom of choice for consumers and producers.\nDisadvantages of a market economy: inequality of income and wealth, public goods will not be provided (non-excludable, non-rivalrous), merit goods will be under-provided, demerit goods will be over-provided, monopolies may develop, and externalities are ignored.\nA COMMAND ECONOMY (centrally planned) is one where the government owns all factors of production and makes all economic decisions through central planning.\nAdvantages: greater equality of income, essential services provided for all, no unemployment (in theory), externalities can be accounted for.\nDisadvantages: lack of consumer choice, inefficiency due to no profit motive, bureaucracy and corruption, no incentive to innovate, shortages and surpluses common due to lack of price signals.\nA MIXED ECONOMY combines elements of both market and command systems. The private sector produces most goods and services, while the government intervenes to correct market failures.\nIreland operates as a MIXED ECONOMY. The private sector is dominant, but the government provides public goods (defence, street lighting), merit goods (education, healthcare), regulates markets (ComReg, CCPC), redistributes income (taxation and social welfare), and manages the macroeconomy.\nThe PRICE MECHANISM (or market mechanism) is the system by which prices act as signals to allocate resources. When demand for a good rises, its price rises, signalling to producers to supply more. When demand falls, price falls, and producers supply less.\nThe price mechanism performs three functions: (1) SIGNALLING function — prices signal changes in market conditions; (2) INCENTIVE function — price changes create incentives for producers and consumers to change behaviour; (3) RATIONING function — prices ration scarce goods to those willing and able to pay.\nMARKET FAILURE occurs when the free market fails to allocate resources efficiently. Causes include: externalities, public goods, merit/demerit goods, imperfect information, monopoly power, and inequality.\nGOVERNMENT INTERVENTION to correct market failure includes: taxation (to discourage demerit goods like tobacco), subsidies (to encourage merit goods), regulation (minimum wage, environmental standards), direct provision (public hospitals), and legislation (competition law).\nIn Ireland, examples of government intervention include: carbon tax (to reduce emissions), minimum wage (€12.70/hr in 2024), sugar tax on sweetened drinks, and the CCPC (Competition and Consumer Protection Commission) to prevent monopoly abuse.\nExaminers frequently ask you to compare two economic systems or discuss the advantages/disadvantages of the mixed economy. Structure your answer clearly with headings and use Irish examples to support your points.\nExam Tip: If asked 'Discuss the merits of a mixed economy,' always acknowledge BOTH the market and government components, and give specific Irish examples of each.",
        keyTerms: ["free market economy", "command economy", "mixed economy", "price mechanism", "market failure", "consumer sovereignty", "public goods", "merit goods", "demerit goods", "externalities", "government intervention"],
        formula: null,
        examTip: "Know the three functions of the price mechanism (signalling, incentive, rationing) — this is a common 15-mark part. Always relate to Ireland as a mixed economy with specific examples."
      },
      {
        title: "Central Concepts: Ceteris Paribus, Positive vs Normative, Margin",
        content: "CETERIS PARIBUS is a Latin phrase meaning 'all other things being equal' or 'all other factors remaining constant.' It is used extensively in economics to isolate the effect of one variable on another.\nFor example, the Law of Demand states: 'As price rises, quantity demanded falls, CETERIS PARIBUS.' This means we assume income, tastes, prices of substitutes/complements, and all other factors stay the same while we examine the effect of a price change.\nWithout ceteris paribus, it would be impossible to analyse cause and effect in economics, because in reality many variables change simultaneously.\nPOSITIVE ECONOMICS deals with objective, factual statements that can be tested and verified. Example: 'Ireland's unemployment rate was 4.3% in 2024.' This is a statement of fact — it is either true or false.\nNORMATIVE ECONOMICS deals with subjective, value-based statements that involve opinions and judgments. Example: 'The government should increase spending on healthcare.' This is a value judgment — it depends on one's priorities and beliefs.\nThe distinction matters because positive statements can be proven or disproven with evidence, while normative statements cannot — they depend on personal or political values.\nEconomic POLICY often involves normative judgments. For example, deciding whether to prioritise reducing inequality or promoting economic growth is a normative choice.\nTHE MARGIN is one of the most important concepts in economics. Decisions are made 'at the margin,' meaning we consider the additional (marginal) benefit versus the additional (marginal) cost of one more unit.\nMARGINAL COST (MC) is the additional cost of producing one more unit of output. MARGINAL BENEFIT (MB) or MARGINAL UTILITY (MU) is the additional satisfaction gained from consuming one more unit.\nRational economic agents (consumers, firms, governments) will continue an activity as long as MARGINAL BENEFIT ≥ MARGINAL COST. The optimal level is where MB = MC.\nFor a firm, profit is maximised where Marginal Cost = Marginal Revenue (MC = MR). For a consumer, utility is maximised where the marginal utility per euro spent is equal across all goods: MU_A/P_A = MU_B/P_B (the equi-marginal principle).\nWorked Example (Marginal Thinking): A student has 4 hours to study. The marginal benefit of the 1st hour of Economics study = 20 marks gained. The 2nd hour = 12 marks. The 3rd hour = 5 marks. The 4th hour = 1 mark. If Maths study yields 8 marks per hour, the student should study Economics for 2 hours (MB > 8) then switch to Maths (where MB = 8 > Economics 3rd hour MB of 5).\nMARGINAL ANALYSIS underpins much of microeconomics: diminishing marginal utility, diminishing marginal returns, marginal cost pricing, and marginal revenue.\nThe concept of the margin distinguishes economics from other social sciences — economists always think in terms of incremental changes rather than all-or-nothing decisions.\nExam Tip: If an exam question asks 'Explain the importance of the margin in economic decision-making,' give the definition of marginal analysis, explain MB = MC as the decision rule, and provide a worked example for both a consumer and a firm.",
        keyTerms: ["ceteris paribus", "positive economics", "normative economics", "marginal cost", "marginal benefit", "marginal utility", "equi-marginal principle", "rational economic agent"],
        formula: "Optimal Decision Rule: Continue activity while MB ≥ MC; stop where MB = MC\nEqui-Marginal Principle: MU_A / P_A = MU_B / P_B",
        examTip: "Ceteris paribus appears in almost every demand/supply question. Marginal analysis is central to consumer theory and firm theory — always frame decisions as 'one more unit' comparisons."
      }
