    subtopics: [
      {
        title: "The Law of Demand",
        content: "DEMAND is the quantity of a good or service that consumers are willing and able to purchase at various prices over a given period of time, ceteris paribus.\nNote the two key conditions: WILLING (they want it) and ABLE (they can afford it). A desire without purchasing power is not effective demand.\nThe LAW OF DEMAND states: as the price of a good rises, the quantity demanded falls, and vice versa, ceteris paribus. This inverse relationship gives the demand curve its downward slope from left to right.\nThere are three reasons for the downward slope: (1) INCOME EFFECT — when price rises, consumers' real income (purchasing power) falls, so they buy less; (2) SUBSTITUTION EFFECT — when price rises, consumers switch to cheaper alternatives; (3) DIMINISHING MARGINAL UTILITY — each additional unit consumed gives less satisfaction, so consumers will only buy more at lower prices.\nINDIVIDUAL DEMAND is one consumer's demand. MARKET DEMAND is the horizontal summation (adding up quantities at each price) of all individual demand curves in a market.\nA DEMAND SCHEDULE is a table showing the quantity demanded at each price. A DEMAND CURVE is the graphical representation, with PRICE on the y-axis and QUANTITY on the x-axis.\nExample Demand Schedule for Crisps in Ireland:\nPrice (€): 3.00, 2.50, 2.00, 1.50, 1.00\nQuantity Demanded (000s): 10, 20, 35, 55, 80\nPlotting these points and joining them gives a downward-sloping demand curve.\nA MOVEMENT ALONG the demand curve occurs when the price of the good itself changes. A fall in price causes an extension (movement down along the curve); a rise in price causes a contraction (movement up along the curve).\nA SHIFT of the demand curve occurs when a non-price factor changes, causing the entire curve to move left (decrease in demand) or right (increase in demand).\nFactors causing a rightward SHIFT (increase) in demand: rise in consumer income (for normal goods), fall in price of a complement, rise in price of a substitute, favourable change in tastes/fashion, increase in population, expectation of future price rises, successful advertising.\nFactors causing a leftward SHIFT (decrease) in demand: fall in income (for normal goods), rise in price of a complement, fall in price of a substitute, unfavourable change in tastes, decrease in population.\nA NORMAL GOOD is one where demand increases when income rises (positive income effect) — e.g., restaurant meals. An INFERIOR GOOD is one where demand decreases when income rises — e.g., own-brand budget products.\nA SUBSTITUTE is a good that can be used in place of another (e.g., Pepsi and Coca-Cola). A COMPLEMENT is a good consumed together with another (e.g., printers and ink cartridges).\nGIFFEN GOODS are a theoretical exception to the Law of Demand — as price rises, quantity demanded also rises. This occurs for essential inferior goods where the income effect outweighs the substitution effect. Example: potatoes during the Irish Famine (Robert Giffen's observation).\nVEBLEN GOODS are another exception — luxury goods where higher prices increase demand because the high price signals status and exclusivity (e.g., designer handbags, Rolex watches).\nExam Tip: Always distinguish between a movement along (caused by own-price change) and a shift of (caused by non-price factors) the demand curve. This distinction is worth significant marks and is one of the most common errors students make.",
        keyTerms: ["demand", "law of demand", "income effect", "substitution effect", "diminishing marginal utility", "demand schedule", "demand curve", "normal good", "inferior good", "substitute", "complement", "Giffen good", "Veblen good"],
        formula: null,
        examTip: "ALWAYS distinguish 'movement along' (own price change) from 'shift of' (non-price factor). Draw diagrams with labelled axes (P and Q), show the direction of the shift with arrows, and label both D1 and D2."
      },
      {
        title: "The Law of Supply",
        content: "SUPPLY is the quantity of a good or service that producers are willing and able to offer for sale at various prices over a given period of time, ceteris paribus.\nThe LAW OF SUPPLY states: as the price of a good rises, the quantity supplied rises, and vice versa, ceteris paribus. This positive (direct) relationship gives the supply curve its upward slope from left to right.\nReasons for the upward slope: (1) Higher prices mean higher potential profits, attracting more producers into the market; (2) Existing producers increase output to earn more revenue; (3) Higher prices cover the higher marginal costs of producing additional units (due to diminishing returns).\nA SUPPLY SCHEDULE is a table showing quantity supplied at each price. A SUPPLY CURVE is the graphical representation, with PRICE on the y-axis and QUANTITY on the x-axis.\nExample Supply Schedule for Artisan Bread in Ireland:\nPrice (€): 2.00, 3.00, 4.00, 5.00, 6.00\nQuantity Supplied (000s): 5, 15, 30, 50, 75\nA MOVEMENT ALONG the supply curve occurs when the price of the good itself changes. A price rise causes an extension (upward movement); a price fall causes a contraction (downward movement).\nA SHIFT of the supply curve occurs when a non-price factor changes. A rightward shift means supply has increased (more supplied at every price). A leftward shift means supply has decreased.\nFactors causing a RIGHTWARD shift (increase in supply): fall in costs of production (e.g., cheaper raw materials), improved technology, government subsidies, favourable weather (agriculture), entry of new firms, fall in price of goods in joint supply.\nFactors causing a LEFTWARD shift (decrease in supply): rise in costs of production (e.g., wage increases, higher energy costs), new taxes or regulations, adverse weather, exit of firms from the market, rise in price of goods in competitive supply.\nJOINT SUPPLY: goods produced together — e.g., beef and leather. If the price of beef rises, supply of leather also increases (rightward shift).\nCOMPETITIVE SUPPLY: goods that use the same resources — e.g., wheat and barley on the same farmland. If the price of wheat rises, farmers switch to wheat, and supply of barley decreases (leftward shift).\nIn Ireland, supply shifts are commonly seen in agriculture (weather-dependent), the housing market (planning regulations and construction costs), and energy markets (carbon taxes increasing costs).\nThe INDIVIDUAL SUPPLY curve shows one firm's supply. The MARKET SUPPLY curve is the horizontal summation of all individual supply curves.\nExceptions to the Law of Supply include: (1) Fixed supply — perfectly inelastic supply such as land, antiques, or concert tickets for a specific event; (2) Backward-bending labour supply curve — beyond a certain wage, workers may choose more leisure over more work.\nExam Tip: When drawing supply curve shifts, always show the original curve S1 and the new curve S2, with an arrow indicating the direction of shift. Label axes P and Q. State the cause of the shift explicitly.",
        keyTerms: ["supply", "law of supply", "supply schedule", "supply curve", "joint supply", "competitive supply", "movement along", "shift of supply"],
        formula: null,
        examTip: "Common exam error: confusing a shift of the supply curve with a movement along it. A change in own price = movement along. A change in costs, technology, taxes, or subsidies = shift."
      },
      {
        title: "Market Equilibrium",
        content: "MARKET EQUILIBRIUM occurs where the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied at a particular price. This price is called the EQUILIBRIUM PRICE (or market-clearing price), and the corresponding quantity is the EQUILIBRIUM QUANTITY.\nAt equilibrium, there is no tendency for price to change — the market is in a state of rest. There is no excess demand and no excess supply.\nDiagrammatically, equilibrium is found at the intersection of the demand curve and the supply curve. Always label this point 'E', and draw dashed lines to both axes to show the equilibrium price (Pe) and equilibrium quantity (Qe).\nEXCESS DEMAND (shortage) occurs when price is BELOW equilibrium. At this price, quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied. Consumers compete for the limited goods, bidding the price up until equilibrium is restored.\nEXCESS SUPPLY (surplus) occurs when price is ABOVE equilibrium. At this price, quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded. Producers cannot sell all their output and reduce prices until equilibrium is restored.\nWorked Example: Suppose in the Irish market for coffee: at €3.50, Qd = 100,000 cups/day and Qs = 60,000 cups/day. Excess demand = 100,000 - 60,000 = 40,000 cups. Price will rise. At €5.00, Qd = 60,000 and Qs = 100,000. Excess supply = 40,000 cups. Price will fall. At €4.00, Qd = Qs = 80,000. This is equilibrium.\nCHANGES IN EQUILIBRIUM occur when demand or supply curves shift. There are four key scenarios:\n(1) Increase in demand (D shifts right): equilibrium price rises, equilibrium quantity rises.\n(2) Decrease in demand (D shifts left): equilibrium price falls, equilibrium quantity falls.\n(3) Increase in supply (S shifts right): equilibrium price falls, equilibrium quantity rises.\n(4) Decrease in supply (S shifts left): equilibrium price rises, equilibrium quantity falls.\nWhen BOTH curves shift simultaneously, the effect on one variable (price or quantity) is determinate but the other is indeterminate (depends on the relative size of the shifts).\nExample: If demand for electric vehicles in Ireland increases (due to government grants) AND supply increases (due to new manufacturers entering the market), quantity definitely rises, but price could rise, fall, or stay the same depending on which shift is larger.\nThe concept of equilibrium is central to understanding how markets work. The PRICE MECHANISM automatically adjusts to clear the market — this is Adam Smith's 'invisible hand.'\nGOVERNMENT INTERFERENCE with equilibrium can cause problems: a PRICE CEILING (maximum price) set below equilibrium causes excess demand (e.g., rent controls in Dublin creating housing shortages). A PRICE FLOOR (minimum price) set above equilibrium causes excess supply (e.g., the EU's Common Agricultural Policy creating butter mountains and wine lakes).\nIreland's MINIMUM WAGE (€12.70/hr in 2024) is a price floor in the labour market. If set above the equilibrium wage, it can cause excess supply of labour (unemployment), particularly among low-skilled workers. However, supporters argue it reduces poverty and exploitation.\nThe LIVING WAGE differs from the minimum wage — it is the minimum income necessary for a worker to meet basic needs. In Ireland, the Living Wage Technical Group estimated it at approximately €14.80/hr in 2024.\nExam Tip: For ANY question on equilibrium changes, ALWAYS draw a before-and-after diagram showing the shift, and clearly state the effect on BOTH price AND quantity. This structured approach maximises marks.",
        keyTerms: ["equilibrium", "equilibrium price", "equilibrium quantity", "excess demand", "excess supply", "price ceiling", "price floor", "minimum wage", "market-clearing price"],
        formula: "Equilibrium Condition: Qd = Qs\nExcess Demand = Qd - Qs (when P < Pe)\nExcess Supply = Qs - Qd (when P > Pe)",
        examTip: "Draw TWO diagrams when asked about equilibrium changes — the original and the new equilibrium. Label everything: D1, D2, S1, S2, E1, E2, P1, P2, Q1, Q2. State effects on BOTH price AND quantity."
      },
      {
        title: "Government Intervention in Markets",
        content: "Governments intervene in markets to correct MARKET FAILURES — situations where the free market fails to allocate resources efficiently or equitably.\nA SUBSIDY is a payment by the government to producers to reduce their costs of production. This shifts the supply curve to the RIGHT, reducing the equilibrium price and increasing the equilibrium quantity.\nDiagram for a subsidy: Draw the original S1 and D curves with equilibrium at E1. Then draw S2 to the right of S1 (the vertical distance between S1 and S2 = the subsidy per unit). New equilibrium E2 has a lower price (P2) and higher quantity (Q2). The subsidy per unit is shared between consumer (P1 - P2) and producer.\nIrish example: The government provides subsidies for renewable energy (wind and solar) through the Renewable Electricity Support Scheme (RESS) to encourage green energy production.\nA TAX (indirect tax/excise duty) is a charge imposed by the government on producers, increasing their costs. This shifts the supply curve to the LEFT, raising the equilibrium price and reducing the equilibrium quantity.\nDiagram for a tax: Draw S1 and D with equilibrium at E1. Draw S2 to the left of S1 (vertical distance = tax per unit). New equilibrium E2 has higher price (P2) and lower quantity (Q2). The tax burden is shared between consumer and producer depending on the relative elasticities of demand and supply.\nThe TAX INCIDENCE (who bears more of the tax burden) depends on elasticity. If demand is inelastic (e.g., cigarettes), consumers bear most of the tax. If demand is elastic, producers bear more.\nIrish examples of indirect taxes: excise duty on cigarettes (€8.23 per pack of 20 in 2024), carbon tax (€56 per tonne of CO2 in 2024), VAT (standard rate 23%), sugar tax on sweetened drinks.\nA PRICE CEILING (maximum price) is a legally imposed maximum price, set BELOW the equilibrium price. Purpose: to make essential goods affordable for consumers.\nEffect of a price ceiling: creates excess demand (Qd > Qs), leading to shortages, black markets, queuing, rationing, and deterioration in quality. Irish example: rent pressure zones (RPZs) in Dublin limit rent increases to 2% per year.\nA PRICE FLOOR (minimum price) is a legally imposed minimum price, set ABOVE the equilibrium price. Purpose: to protect producers' incomes.\nEffect of a price floor: creates excess supply (Qs > Qd). In product markets, the government may buy up the surplus (e.g., EU CAP intervention buying). In labour markets, the minimum wage can cause unemployment if set above the market wage.\nIrish example: The Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) historically set intervention prices for agricultural products, guaranteeing farmers a minimum price. This led to overproduction ('butter mountains,' 'wine lakes').\nOther forms of government intervention include: QUOTAS (limits on quantity — e.g., milk quotas under CAP until 2015, import quotas), REGULATION (environmental standards, safety standards, competition law), and DIRECT PROVISION (state provision of healthcare through the HSE, education).\nThe government also uses FISCAL POLICY (taxation and government spending) and MONETARY POLICY (interest rates, managed by the ECB for Ireland as a eurozone member) to manage the overall economy.\nExam Tip: For subsidy/tax questions, the diagram is essential. Show S1, S2, the vertical distance (= subsidy or tax per unit), and clearly mark the price the consumer pays, the price the producer receives, the deadweight loss (if applicable), and the government cost/revenue.",
        keyTerms: ["subsidy", "indirect tax", "price ceiling", "price floor", "tax incidence", "market failure", "quota", "deadweight loss", "fiscal policy", "CAP"],
        formula: "Government Revenue from Tax = Tax per unit × Quantity sold\nGovernment Cost of Subsidy = Subsidy per unit × Quantity sold\nTax Incidence: Consumer burden = P2 - P1; Producer burden = (P1 + Tax) - P2",
        examTip: "Always draw the diagram for subsidies/taxes. Show the shift in supply, the new equilibrium, and how the burden is shared. For price ceilings/floors, show the gap between Qd and Qs and label it as excess demand or excess supply."
      }
    ]
