        content: "Fiscal policy refers to the use of government spending and taxation to influence the overall level of economic activity in the economy.\nIt is one of the two main macroeconomic policy tools available to governments, the other being monetary policy.\nIn Ireland, fiscal policy is controlled by the Irish Government through the annual Budget, which is typically announced in October each year.\nThe Minister for Finance sets out plans for government revenue (taxation) and government expenditure (spending) for the coming year.\nThe key objective of fiscal policy is to promote economic stability, sustainable growth, full employment, and price stability.\nFiscal policy can be expansionary (increasing spending or cutting taxes to stimulate the economy) or contractionary (reducing spending or raising taxes to cool an overheating economy).\nSince Ireland is a member of the eurozone, it cannot use monetary policy independently, so fiscal policy becomes an even more important tool for managing the domestic economy.\nHowever, Ireland's fiscal policy is constrained by EU fiscal rules, particularly the Stability and Growth Pact (SGP), which requires member states to keep their budget deficit below 3% of GDP and government debt below 60% of GDP.\nThe Fiscal Advisory Council (IFAC) was established in 2011 to provide independent assessment of Ireland's fiscal policy and official budgetary forecasts.\nDuring the Celtic Tiger period (1995-2007), Ireland ran budget surpluses, but after the 2008 financial crisis, large budget deficits emerged, peaking at over 30% of GDP in 2010 due to bank bailout costs.\nThe National Development Plan (NDP) outlines the government's capital spending priorities over a multi-year period.\nA balanced budget occurs when government revenue equals government expenditure.\nA budget surplus occurs when revenue exceeds expenditure, while a budget deficit occurs when expenditure exceeds revenue.\nThe national debt is the cumulative total of all past budget deficits minus any surpluses, representing the total amount the government owes.\nAutomatic stabilisers are built-in fiscal mechanisms (like unemployment benefits and progressive taxation) that naturally counteract economic fluctuations without deliberate policy action.",
        keyTerms: ["Fiscal policy", "Budget deficit", "Budget surplus", "Balanced budget", "National debt", "Expansionary fiscal policy", "Contractionary fiscal policy", "Stability and Growth Pact", "Automatic stabilisers", "Fiscal Advisory Council"],
        formula: "Budget Position = Total Government Revenue - Total Government Expenditure. If positive = surplus; if negative = deficit.",
