        content: "GDP per capita is the most basic measure of development, but it has significant limitations: it does not account for income distribution, the informal economy, non-market activity (such as subsistence farming and domestic work), environmental degradation, or quality of life.\nThe Human Development Index (HDI) was developed by the UN Development Programme (UNDP) as a broader measure of development. It was first published in 1990.\nThe HDI combines three dimensions: health (measured by life expectancy at birth), education (measured by mean years of schooling and expected years of schooling), and standard of living (measured by GNI per capita in PPP dollars).\nEach dimension is indexed on a scale from 0 to 1, and the HDI is the geometric mean of the three dimension indices. A higher HDI indicates greater human development.\nCountries are classified as: very high human development (HDI >= 0.800), high (0.700-0.799), medium (0.550-0.699), and low (below 0.550).\nIreland consistently ranks very highly on the HDI, typically in the top 5-10 countries globally, reflecting high life expectancy, strong educational attainment, and high income levels.\nHowever, the HDI has its own limitations: it does not capture inequality within countries, political freedoms, environmental sustainability, personal security, or cultural factors.\nThe Inequality-adjusted HDI (IHDI) adjusts the HDI for inequality in each dimension. Countries with high inequality see a significant drop from their HDI to their IHDI.\nThe Gender Development Index (GDI) measures gender inequalities in the three HDI dimensions, comparing female and male achievements.\nThe Gender Inequality Index (GII) measures gender-based disadvantage in reproductive health, empowerment, and economic activity.\nThe Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI) identifies people who are poor across multiple dimensions simultaneously, looking at deprivations in health, education, and living standards.\nPurchasing Power Parity (PPP) adjusts income measures to account for differences in price levels between countries, allowing more meaningful international comparisons.\nOther indicators of development include: the Gini coefficient (inequality), literacy rates, infant mortality rates, access to clean water and sanitation, mobile phone and internet penetration, CO2 emissions per capita, and the proportion of the population living below the poverty line.\nNo single indicator can fully capture the complexity of development, which is why composite indices like the HDI are useful despite their limitations.\nWhen evaluating development, it is important to look at trends over time rather than just snapshots, and to consider multiple indicators together for a comprehensive picture."
