        title: "Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) and Ireland",
        content: "FDI occurs when a firm invests significantly in a business in another country (typically 10%+ ownership or greenfield), distinguished from portfolio investment by control and long-term commitment.\nIreland has been exceptionally successful at attracting FDI, particularly from the US. Major investors include Apple, Google, Meta, Microsoft, Intel, Pfizer, and Johnson & Johnson.\nThe IDA Ireland is the state agency responsible for attracting FDI, marketing Ireland as a location and providing support.\nReasons Ireland attracts FDI: low corporation tax (12.5%, moving to 15% under OECD Pillar Two), EU Single Market access (440+ million consumers), young educated English-speaking workforce, eurozone membership, strong legal framework, cluster effects, and quality of life.\nBenefits: job creation (IDA companies employ 300,000+), technology transfer, increased tax revenue, export development, infrastructure improvement, and workforce upskilling.\nConcerns about FDI dependency: profit repatriation (reducing GNP vs GDP), vulnerability to foreign HQ decisions, crowding out indigenous firms, distortion of national accounts, and limited domestic linkages.\nGDP includes multinational output; GNP subtracts repatriated profits. Modified GNI (GNI*) gives a more accurate picture.\nIreland's FDI concentrates in pharma, tech, financial services, and medical devices, unevenly distributed (Dublin, Cork, Galway, Limerick).\nThe OECD BEPS initiative and global minimum tax may affect Ireland, though advantages extend beyond tax.\nEnterprise Ireland supports indigenous firms. Balanced policy requires developing both foreign and Irish-owned businesses.\nIreland's FDI success demonstrates the importance of long-term, consistent policy."
