        title: "Population and Development",
        content: "Population growth affects development through labour supply, demand for goods and services, pressure on resources, and the dependency ratio.\nThe world population reached 8 billion in 2022 and continues to grow, though the growth rate has slowed since the late 1960s.\nThe demographic transition model has four stages: Stage 1 (high birth/death rates, low growth), Stage 2 (high births, falling deaths, rapid growth), Stage 3 (falling births, low deaths, slowing growth), Stage 4 (low births/deaths, low or zero growth).\nDeveloping countries are typically in Stages 2-3; developed countries in Stage 4 or declining.\nMalthus (1798) argued population grows geometrically while food supply grows arithmetically, predicting famine. He did not foresee agricultural technology, contraception, or the demographic transition.\nNeo-Malthusian concerns about finite resources (water, energy, arable land) remain relevant.\nHigh population growth can hinder development by increasing the dependency ratio, straining services, reducing savings per capita, causing environmental damage, and overcrowding cities.\nPopulation growth can also support development through a larger labour force, bigger domestic market, and demographic dividend.\nThe dependency ratio measures the non-working-age population (under 15, over 65) relative to the working-age population.\nIreland has a young population by EU standards but faces ageing challenges: rising pension/healthcare costs, shrinking labour force, and skill shortages. Immigration helps mitigate these.\nIreland's population was shaped by historical emigration (the Famine era) and recent immigration (Celtic Tiger and after).\nNet migration is key for Ireland: inward migration fills skill gaps and supports growth but pressures housing and services.\nPolicies can be pro-natalist (child benefit, parental leave) or anti-natalist (China's one-child policy). Ireland's are generally pro-natalist.\nInvesting in education and healthcare for women and girls is one of the most effective ways to manage population growth and promote development."
