        title: "Globalisation and Development",
        content: "Globalisation is the process of increasing interconnectedness and interdependence between countries through trade, investment, technology, migration, and cultural exchange.\nThe main drivers of globalisation include: advances in transport and communication technology (internet, containerisation, air travel), trade liberalisation (reduction of tariffs and barriers through the WTO and free trade agreements), deregulation of financial markets, the growth of multinational corporations (MNCs), and political changes (fall of communism, EU enlargement).\nGlobalisation has led to a massive increase in international trade, with global exports growing from around 12% of world GDP in 1960 to over 25% today.\nBenefits of globalisation include: access to larger markets, lower prices through increased competition, transfer of technology and knowledge, increased FDI flows to developing countries, greater consumer choice, and cultural exchange.\nGlobalisation has contributed to lifting hundreds of millions of people out of extreme poverty, particularly in East and South-East Asia (China, Vietnam, South Korea).\nHowever, globalisation has also created losers: workers in developed countries who have lost jobs to cheaper labour abroad (offshoring), domestic firms unable to compete with multinational competitors, environmental damage from increased production and transport, cultural homogenisation, and increased vulnerability to global economic shocks.\nThe 2008 global financial crisis demonstrated how interconnected financial markets can transmit economic problems rapidly across borders. Ireland was severely affected due to its open, globally connected economy.\nMultinational corporations (MNCs) are central agents of globalisation. They can bring investment, jobs, and technology to host countries, but they can also exploit weak regulations, avoid taxes through transfer pricing, and exert excessive influence on government policy.\nGlobalisation and inequality: while globalisation has reduced inequality between countries (as developing countries grow faster), it may have increased inequality within countries (as the benefits accrue disproportionately to skilled workers, capital owners, and urban populations).\nThe race to the bottom refers to the concern that countries competing for FDI may lower tax rates, weaken labour protections, and relax environmental standards, ultimately harming workers and the environment.\nIreland is often cited as a major beneficiary of globalisation: its open economy, EU membership, English-speaking workforce, and attractive tax regime have made it a hub for multinational activity.\nHowever, Ireland's dependence on globalisation also creates vulnerabilities: changes in US tax policy, global trade disputes, or a shift in multinational strategy could significantly affect the Irish economy.\nThe COVID-19 pandemic exposed the risks of globalised supply chains, as disruptions in one country caused shortages worldwide. This led to discussions about reshoring (bringing production back home) and supply chain diversification.\nDigital globalisation through the internet, e-commerce, and remote work has opened new opportunities for developing countries to participate in the global economy, particularly in services.\nManaging globalisation requires international cooperation on trade rules, tax policy, environmental standards, and labour rights to ensure that the benefits are widely shared and the costs are minimised."
